fic reports about Rubin Ultra and rack-scale delays add downside risk to the roughly $553–554 billion reference-based estimate, while Nvidia’s brief roadmap statement provides a counterpoint. The new reports do not quantify an FY2028 revenue reduction sufficient to move the central estimate below $550 billion.\",\n  \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n      \"view_id\": \"v13\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"view_id\": \"v15\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"view_id\": \"v4\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"view_id\": \"v8\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reference\": \"Wall Street analysts’ estimate of about $554 billion for the following fiscal year; this is rung 2 ($550 billion to below $650 billion).\",\n  \"implied_outcome\": \"About $553 billion, derived by carrying the cited 41% growth estimate forward from $392 billion ($392 billion × 1.41). That is just above the $550 billion rung-2 floor; the reported product and rack risks are not quantified enough to imply a central estimate below it.\"\n}",
